-- ============================================================================
-- DR7 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Cementing, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: placement. The flow path is parametrised by PUMPED
-- VOLUME rather than by depth, because depth is not single valued on a U, and
-- pumping one cubic metre advances every front by exactly one cubic metre of
-- path coordinate wherever it is. The fronts partition the path into intervals
-- whose lengths always sum to the path length. Two maps convert volume back to
-- measured depth, the annulus one running BACKWARDS from the shoe. And the
-- whole pressure calculation is one line: the maximum of zero and the annulus
-- head plus friction less the inside head, with the heads on the exact
-- minimum-curvature true vertical depths and the friction imported from the
-- hydraulics loss kernel rather than reimplemented here.
--
-- THE FINDING THE TIER IS BUILT ON. The pump rate is a straight TRADE. Faster
-- adds friction, which supports the annular column and cures free fall, and
-- the same friction raises the equivalent circulating density at the
-- shallowest exposed formation. One knob, two constraints, opposite senses.
-- Both edges are BISECTED rather than swept, because a sweep gives an interval
-- and the answer is going to be quoted. On the HORIZONTAL well with a NEAT
-- single slurry, at a previous-shoe limit of 1700 kg/m3, the window is CLOSED:
-- the slowest rate with no free fall is 0.02059793163869393 and the fastest
-- under the limit is 0.017832442077937685, a width of
-- -0.0027654895607562464. Every rate slow enough to protect the shoe free
-- falls and every rate fast enough to prevent free fall fractures it.
--
-- AND THE LEAD SLURRY OPENS IT. The same well with a lighter lead across the
-- cased annulus gives 0.019340962816243038 and 0.024740241221207958, a width
-- of 0.00539927840496492. One change to the programme moved BOTH edges in the
-- helpful direction: the free-fall edge down because the annular column is
-- heavier, and the ECD edge up because the head at the previous shoe is lower.
-- Which is the whole argument for a lead slurry, and it is not the cost one.
--
-- A SECOND FINDING, IN m03. The equivalent circulating density at the CASING
-- shoe on the horizontal well is 1837.9218199066345 kg/m3 before anything has
-- been pumped, against 1646.773401667299 on the slant well, because it divides
-- the friction of a 1600 m lateral by 1214.859173174059 m of true vertical
-- depth. ECD divides a quantity generated along the hole by one measured
-- vertically, and the two stop being proportional the moment the hole turns.
--
-- A THIRD, IN m05, AND IT IS ABOUT THE GRID. The horizontal well's neat
-- programme free falls at exactly ONE of the 61 default steps. Refined to 600
-- the dip spans sixteen samples over about two cubic metres and the worst
-- deficit is -110300.25230836123 Pa rather than -104394.27505085245. The
-- finding survives and one of its digits does not, and neither is visible from
-- a single run.
--
-- Numeric sweep and tier-leakage sweep as for the Associate tier: 0
-- unresolved literals, 0 downward leaks, 0 same-tier answer prints.
--
-- Answer keys live only here and in academy_quiz_questions, which has no
-- client SELECT policy.
-- ============================================================================

values ('cementing', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-the-flow-path","title":"The Flow Path","lesson_keys":["l01-plug-flow-and-what-it-assumes","l02-the-volume-coordinate","l03-fronts-and-intervals","l04-from-volume-to-measured-depth"]},{"key":"m02-the-u-tube","title":"The U-tube","lesson_keys":["l01-three-terms-and-a-maximum","l02-head-on-the-exact-tvd","l03-friction-from-the-loss-kernel","l04-free-fall","l05-the-one-pascal-deadband"]},{"key":"m03-the-job-over-time","title":"The Job Over Time","lesson_keys":["l01-reading-a-pump-pressure-curve","l02-it-falls-before-it-rises","l03-ecd-at-the-previous-shoe","l04-ecd-at-the-shoe","l05-the-end-of-the-job"]},{"key":"m04-two-programmes-one-well","title":"Two Programmes, One Well","lesson_keys":["l01-neat-and-lead-and-tail","l02-what-the-lead-slurry-buys","l03-the-rate-is-a-trade","l04-when-the-window-closes","l05-choosing-a-programme"]},{"key":"m05-reading-a-placement-result","title":"Reading a Placement Result","lesson_keys":["l01-achieved-toc","l02-the-warnings-one-at-a-time","l03-what-the-simulation-cannot-see","l04-sixty-steps"]},{"key":"m06-the-professional-reading","title":"The Professional Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
